            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
              it is true that a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is an equivalence precisely if for all global points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y : * \to Y
              the corresponding homotopy fiber of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is contractible. The same simple statement is not available
              %em in the “external” logic
              for a general
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              may not even have enough global points (it may be non-trivial and have no global point).
            %p
              The above is useful in that it says that
              %em
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
              of the
              = succeed "-category," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
              is true generally, after all.
          %h2#Induction Homotopy algebras over homotopy monads
          %p
            We have seen that homtopy type theory naturally describes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            and some other finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limits"} (∞,1)-limits
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            notion,
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            that is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
          %p
            Traditionally inductive types are in category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreted
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+an+endofunctor"} algebras over an endofunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebra over a monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+endofunctor"} algebra over an endofunctor
            may be thought of as a monad-algebra over a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monad"} free monad
          %p
            However, after generalization to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            this is rectified: here the homotopy-theoretic notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            turns out to be about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-algebras+over+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-monad"} ∞-algebras over an (∞,1)-monad
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"} higher inductive type
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebras+of+a+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-monad"} initial algebras of a presentable (∞,1)-monad
